  "set_proactivity": "Change how often this bot speaks up. Call this only when the user explicitly asks. anchorMessageId must be that user_input message. level is quiet, low, standard, high, or muted. This changes the current bot only.",
  "spawn_session": "Open one work session for this project. Anchor it to a canonical user_input from the current user turn. An ordinary event wake cannot create new tasks from historical messages. Objective wakes require the host-approved objective action. Give a short title, a brief, one to eight success criteria, a kind, and whether the session is read-only. The runtime freezes models and starts the session; do not claim the work is finished from this call. Pass supersedes with the old session id when this session replaces one: the runtime stops the old session and starts this one. Do not set a disposition field; the runtime derives it from the call.\n\nScheduling is host-owned: each project admits one in-place write and two readonly sessions; actual worktree writes have a separate workspace. Global model rounds share a hard configured limit. priority is high, normal (default), or low. High requires an anchorMessageIds entry from the current user turn explicitly requesting urgency or high priority; wake or historical input cannot authorize high. dependsOn must name existing sessions in this project; every dependency must be accepted before execution. Failed dependencies require a user decision. A queued response means the session has not started.\n\nisolation is auto (default), none, or worktree. Read-only and non-Git sessions run in place. Auto isolates Git writes when the workspace is dirty or another writer is running. Explicit none waits for the in-place write slot; worktree creation failure or insufficient disk keeps the task queued. Only the host's verified worktree proves isolation. Acceptance and merge-back are separate host facts: policy acceptance does not authorize a merge unless the user enabled that project policy. Merge-back never pushes a remote.\n\nWhen supersedes is set, the previous task is paused and its work is retained. Only active tasks in this project can be replaced; result-ready or accepted tasks cannot. Use resume_session to restore an old task.",
